menu "BlueAudio Puck: buttons and LED"

    comment "Buttons are active low: wire each between its pin and GND."

    config PUCK_BT1_GPIO
        int "BT1 GPIO -- next track, hold for volume up (-1 if not fitted)"
        default 32
        range -1 39
        help
            Internal pull-up, active low. No external resistor needed.

            Avoid GPIO 34-39: those pins are input-only AND have no internal
            pull-up, so a button on them floats unless you fit your own
            resistor.

    config PUCK_BT2_GPIO
        int "BT2 GPIO -- play/pause, hold to pair (-1 if not fitted)"
        default 33
        range -1 39
        help
            The main button. GPIO 33 is an RTC pin, which is why this one is
            the deep-sleep wake source: ext0 can only watch a single pin, so
            the button people reach for first gets it.

    config PUCK_BT3_GPIO
        int "BT3 GPIO -- previous track, hold for volume down (-1 if not fitted)"
        default 27
        range -1 39

    config PUCK_LED_GPIO
        int "Status LED GPIO (-1 if not fitted)"
        default -1
        range -1 33
        help
            Driven by LEDC PWM so the LED can breathe and dim rather than only
            blink. Must be an output-capable pin: GPIO 34-39 are input only.

            Defaults to "not fitted" because GPIO 2 -- the on-board LED on most
            devkits -- carries I2S data. With the OLED fitted the LED is
            largely redundant anyway, since the screen shows the same states in
            words. Note GPIO 27 is BT3 by default, so pick something else.

    config PUCK_LED_ACTIVE_LOW
        bool "Status LED is active low"
        default n
        help
            Enable if the LED is wired between the pin and 3V3 rather than
            between the pin and ground.

    config PUCK_VOLUME_STEP
        int "Volume change per repeat while a volume button is held"
        default 6
        range 1 32
        help
            In AVRCP units, where full scale is 127. Held buttons repeat every
            180 ms, so 6 sweeps the whole range in about four seconds -- fast
            enough to be useful, slow enough to stop where you meant to.

endmenu
